1. Download VSCode connect to CISCO Secure Client VPN. 
2. In VSCode, download the "Remote-SSH" extension.
3. Access "Remote-SSH". Select "Connect to Host..." >> "Configure New SSH Hosts..." >> "*/.ssh/config"
4. In the config file, input:

#Head node access  
Host keeling
    HostName keeling9.earth.illinois.edu
    User username
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 #to bypass password entry. key needs to be set up locally in authorized keys.  
    ForwardAgent yes  #allows forwarding to the compute node

# Compute node wildcard access set up
Host keeling-* #for randomized compute nodes
    User username
    ProxyJump keeling
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

5. Connect to keeling ssh to receive access to the keeling head node. All directories should open. 
6. Open the terminal and it should show you bash in the keeling head node. Intialize a the compute node with qlogin (ex. "qlogin -p node -n 4 -mem 72G -time 24:00:00").
7. Once the compute node is activated it will return the name of the compute node. For example, "keeling-g02". This does not mean the current window is connected to the compute node. 
8. Open "Remote-SSH" and "Connect to Host..." and add "ssh keeling-g02". In the config file, it will automatically update the add the new host name. Save the config file. 
9. Open "Remote-SSH" and connect to "keeling-g02".
10. You will now be in the compute node. 
